package pages

import "saui/templates/layout"

templ Stack(csrfToken string) {
	@layout.Base("Stack", csrfToken, "/stack", "/static/css/stack.css") {
		<section class="page-hero">
			<div class="page-hero__content">
				<h1>Stack</h1>
				<p class="lead">{ `Go standard library, SQLite, htmx, and templ. No framework. No ORM. No bundler. A single deployable binary.` }</p>
			</div>
		</section>
		<article>
			<section>
				<h2>Backend</h2>
				<h3>Go: standard library first</h3>
				<p>{ `net/http handles routing and serving. No Gin, no Echo, no Chi.
					The standard library multiplexer gained method and wildcard routing in Go 1.22,
					removing the last practical justification for a routing framework in most applications.` }</p>
				<p>
					{ `The html/template package provides auto-escaping that prevents XSS by construction.
					This project uses ` }<code>templ</code>{ ` instead, for compile-time type safety across
					the many htmx partials. The compiled output is standard Go.` }
				</p>
				<h3>SQLite via modernc.org/sqlite</h3>
				<p>{ `Pure Go SQLite: no cgo, no external shared library. The database is compiled into
					the binary. Deployment is a single file copy. SQLite is the reference implementation
					choice. It is not a requirement of the SAUI pattern.` }</p>
				<p>{ `WAL mode is enabled at startup. WAL allows concurrent reads without blocking writes,
					which is appropriate for a read-heavy content site with occasional state mutations.
					A single writer is enforced; the SQLite concurrency model requires it and the
					append-only event log is naturally sequential.` }</p>
				<h3>What SAUI requires from a persistence layer</h3>
				<p>{ `Three things. An append-only event log: events are inserted, never updated or deleted.
					Sequential projection reads: SELECT ... ORDER BY id ASC to derive current state from
					the log. Serialised writes: concurrent mutations must be ordered so no two events
					are applied simultaneously to the same session.` }</p>
				<p>{ `SQLite satisfies the third by construction. Any database satisfies it explicitly.
					The event log schema, the Action interface, and all projection queries are plain SQL
					and are database-agnostic.` }</p>
				<h3>Adapting to Postgres</h3>
				<p>{ `Replace modernc.org/sqlite with pgx. Remove WAL setup. In the gateway Dispatch
					method, acquire a Postgres advisory lock before the action is applied and release
					it after the event is appended. Everything else is unchanged.` }</p>
				<pre><code>{ `-- Serialise writes per session; released automatically on transaction commit
SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext($1));  -- $1 = session_id

-- Event log schema: same structure, database-native types
CREATE TABLE events (
    id          BIGSERIAL    PRIMARY KEY,
    session_id  TEXT         NOT NULL,
    type        TEXT         NOT NULL,
    payload     JSONB        NOT NULL,
    occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ  NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);` }</code></pre>
				<p>{ `MySQL and MariaDB use GET_LOCK() for the same purpose. The lock scope
					changes; the gateway contract does not. Any database that supports
					row-level locking or advisory locks can satisfy the SAUI write model.` }</p>
				<h3>SSE for real-time</h3>
				<p>{ `Server-Sent Events over standard net/http. No WebSocket unless a use case genuinely
					requires bidirectional framing. SSE is unidirectional, trivial to implement,
					and reconnects automatically. It is sufficient for pushing projection updates to the client.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>Frontend</h2>
				<h3>htmx</h3>
				<p>{ `htmx intercepts form submissions and link clicks, sends them as AJAX requests,
					and swaps the returned HTML fragment into the DOM. The server decides what HTML to return.
					The client decides where to put it.` }</p>
				<p>{ `This is the only JS dependency. It is loaded from a local file with no CDN dependency
					for functionality, pinned to a specific version and verified with a subresource integrity hash.` }</p>
				<h3>Vanilla JS: Baseline 2023</h3>
				<p>{ `No framework. No build step. No bundler. JS files are served directly.
					ES modules via type="module". JSDoc type annotations for IDE inference without a compilation step.` }</p>
				<p>{ `Target: modern evergreen browsers. The audience is developers. No polyfills.` }</p>
				<h3>Vanilla CSS</h3>
				<p>{ `CSS custom properties as design tokens. Grid and Flexbox for layout.
					Container queries for component-level responsiveness. No CSS-in-JS.
					No preprocessor. No utility framework.` }</p>
			</section>
			<section>
				<h2>Deployment</h2>
				<p>{ `A single Go binary. SQLite database on a persistent volume. Fly.io manages TLS.
					No external runtime dependencies. No database server. No cache layer.
					No CDN required for correctness.` }</p>
				<pre><code>{ `# build
go build -o saui .

# deploy
fly deploy` }</code></pre>
				<p>{ `The entire production stack fits in one process. Operational complexity is proportional
					to what the application actually requires, not to what the architecture assumes.` }</p>
				<p><a href="/cases">See the architecture applied to real domains</a></p>
			</section>
		</article>
	}
}
